{"results":[{"url":"https://www.propublica.org/","final_url":"https://www.propublica.org/","title":"ProPublica — Investigative Journalism and News in the Public Interest","description":"ProPublica is an independent, non-profit newsroom that produces investigative journalism in the public interest.","language":"en","text":"## Missouri Voters Just Made It Much Harder for Lawmakers to Overturn Citizen-Led Initiatives\n\nFor years, Missouri Republicans worked to undo or restrict voter-approved laws and amendments. In passing Amendment 6, voters have not only protected the ballot initiative process but also dramatically strengthened their power over the legislature.\n\n## Trump’s State Department Is Giving $1 Million to Controversial Group to Study Crimes Against White South Africans\n\nThe new award, the administration’s latest show of support for the Afrikaner cause, comes after it cut human rights funding to nations in crisis. One former diplomat called the move “offensive.”\n\n## SafeSport Will Now Alert Schools, Clubs if Coaches Sanctioned for Abuse Continue to Work With Kids\n\nThe U.S. Center for SafeSport is taking new steps to protect children from athletic coaches banned over abuse allegations. The move follows a ProPublica-Washington Post investigation that found several sanctioned coaches continued to work with kids.\n\n## Vermont Has Spent Nearly a Billion Dollars to Clean Up Lake Champlain. But the Green Slime Keeps Coming Back.\n\nVermont kicked off an ambitious effort in 2015 to clean up waterways and meet tougher EPA restrictions. But after a decade and nearly a billion dollars spent, potentially toxic algae still lingers in Lake Champlain. What happened?\n\nVermont Was Supposed to Get an Audit of Its Lake Champlain Cleanup. It Never Happened.\n\nDo You Live Near Lake Champlain? Help Seven Days and ProPublica Investigate Algae Blooms.\n\n## I Was There to Document the Aftermath of Deadly Flooding. Then, It Started Raining.\n\nThree people died in Ruidoso, New Mexico, last year when floods swept through the area. As the mayor prioritized tourism after the flood recovery, first responders said they weren’t equipped. Our visual journalist documented the unfolding story.\n\nTown Leaders Said They Were Prepared for Floods. First Responders Disagree.\n\n## Inside Trump’s Futile Hunt for Noncitizen Voters\n\nClaiming that hundreds of thousands of noncitizens were voting illegally, Trump tasked agents who usually investigate cross-border crime with uncovering proof. We followed along to find out: How many of these voters could there actually be?\n\nProPublica is a nonprofit, investigative newsroom that exposes corruption. We report in all 50 states and partner with local newsrooms. Our work spurs real-world impact and has received numerous awards, including nine Pulitzer Prizes. Read more\n\n## Browse Topics\n\nSkip topics list\n\n## The Dominican Baseball Factory\n\nThe Ugly Truth Behind MLB’s Recruiting Paradise\n\nIn a country where baseball is king, a corrosive system preys on young players’ dreams of major league stardom and financial security.\n\n### The Dominican Baseball Factory\n\nTeams make handshake deals with players as young as 11 while moneylenders prey on their families. The industry is corrupt, but Major League Baseball and the players union have done little about it.\n\n### The Rising Baseball Star, a Fatal Car Crash and the Fixer\n\nWhen an accident involving top prospect Oneil Cruz left three dead on a darkened road in the Dominican Republic, secret payoffs helped rescue his career and launched a shadowy business empire.\n\n## Events\n\n### Making America Healthy? Examining the Raw Claims and Real Risks of MAHA.\n\nJoin us for a special conversation with ProPublica reporters investigating the “Make America Healthy Again” movement and how it is putting children at risk.\n\nNov. 10 4-5 p.m. EST\n\nVirtual Event\n\n## Life of the Mother\n\nHow Abortion Bans Lead to Preventable Deaths\n\nWhen the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022, doctors warned that women would die, but lawmakers who passed state abortion bans didn’t listen. The worst consequences are now becoming clear.\n\n### Ectopic Pregnancy Deaths Have Nearly Doubled. It’s Worse in States With Abortion Bans.\n\nWith prompt and appropriate medical care, experts say, people shouldn’t die from an ectopic pregnancy. Women in Texas and Oklahoma have sued over delays in care, which, coupled with our new analysis, raises questions about the role of abortion bans.\n\n### “Ticking Time Bomb”: A Pregnant Mother Kept Getting Sicker. She Died After She Couldn’t Get an Abortion in Texas.\n\nProPublica has found multiple cases of women with underlying health conditions who died when they couldn’t access abortions. Tierra Walker, a 37-year-old mother, was told by doctors there was no emergency before preeclampsia killed her.\n\n## Explore Our Data\n\nMaps, Visualizations, and Databases\n\n* ### Nonprofit Explorer: Research Tax-Exempt Organizations\n\n  Search the full text of 18 million tax filing documents going back as far as 2001.\n* ### Looking at Nursing Homes? See Which Ones Have Been Cited for Deficiencies\n* ### Explore Financial Disclosures From President Trump and 2,300 of His Appointees\n* ### Look Up Where Your Generic Prescription Drugs Were Made\n* ### Tracking Habeas Cases","author":null,"published_date":"2026-11-05T11:01:00+00:00","latency_ms":737.0374180027284,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}